Creating a fake login page for security training is a great way to educate users about phishing risks. Below is a simple, safe example of a **fake login page** designed to mimic real websites. This page is **not functional** and will not collect any real data. It’s strictly for **educational purposes** only.

---

### ✅ **Important Notes**
- **Do not use this for real websites** or on actual users.
- This page is **static** and requires a local server (e.g., Python HTTP server) to view.
- Always verify the URL and website legitimacy before entering sensitive information.

---

### 🧩 **Fake Login Page Code (HTML + CSS + JavaScript)**

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8" />
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
  <title>SecureLogin - Fake Page</title>
  <style>
    body {
      margin: 0;
      padding: 0;
      font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
      background: #111;
      color: #fff;
      display: flex;
      justify-content: center;
      align-items: center;
      height: 100vh;
    }

    .login-container {
      background: #222;
      padding: 40px 30px;
      border-radius: 10px;
      box-shadow: 0 0 15px #000;
      text-align: center;
    }

    .login-container h2 {
      margin-bottom: 20px;
    }

    .login-container input {
      width: 100%;
      padding: 12px;
      margin: 10px 0;
      border: none;
      border-radius: 5px;
      font-size: 16px;
    }

    .login-container button {
      padding: 12px 20px;
      background: #ff4444;
      border: none;
      border-radius: 5px;
      color: white;
      font-size: 16px;
      cursor: pointer;
    }

    .login-container button:hover {
      background: #cc0000;
    }

    .message {
      margin-top: 20px;
      font-size: 14px;
      color: #ff4444;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <div class="login-container">
    <h2>SecureLogin</h2>
    <form id="loginForm">
      <input type="text" placeholder="Username" required />
      <input type="password" placeholder="Password" required />
      <button type="submit">Login</button>
    </form>
    <div class="message" id="message">This is a fake login page for training. Do not enter real credentials.</div>
  </div>

  <script>
    document.getElementById('loginForm').addEventListener('submit', function (e) {
      e.preventDefault();
      document.getElementById('message').textContent = "You clicked the login button on a fake page. This is for training purposes only.";
    });
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

---

### 🔧 **How to Use**
1. **Save the code** as `fake-login.html`.
2. **Open it in a browser** (e.g., `file://` or a local server).
3. **Test it** with colleagues or students to demonstrate phishing risks.

---

### 🛡️ **Red Flags to Highlight**
- **Suspicious URL**: `https://phishing.example.com` (fake domain).
- **Poor design**: Minimal padding, dark background, and lack of real website branding.
- **No "About" or "Privacy Policy" links**.
- **No SSL certificate** (look for `https://` with a valid certificate in real sites).

---

### 🚫 **Do Not Do This**
- Never share this page with real users.
- Avoid using it to test real websites or networks.
- Always verify the legitimacy of websites before entering credentials.

---

